---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import '../../styles/ctf.css';
import Cmd from '../../components/Cmd.astro';
import { coreCommands, extraCommands, ctfRepo } from '../../lib/termux-ctf';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/+$/, '') + '/';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="How to play the Termux CTF"
  description="A reference for every command, a worked example from start to finish, and what to do when a flag is rejected."
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="ctf-hero">
      <h1>How to play</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Seven commands, and you will use four of them. They behave the same way for every
        challenge, so the first one teaches you the whole tool. This page is the reference:
        keep it open in another tab while you play.
      </p>
      <div class="chip-row">
        <span class="chip">4 core commands</span>
        <span class="chip">3 for when you need them</span>
        <span class="chip chip--mono">6 challenges, 120 points</span>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section>
      <h2>Before you begin</h2>
      <p>
        The tool is installed once and then stays out of the way. Three things should be
        true before you start.
      </p>
      <ol class="ctf-steps">
        <li>
          <strong>Termux is installed</strong>
          <span>From F-Droid, not the Play Store, which ships a version that no longer receives updates.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The tool is installed</strong>
          <span>Four commands, listed on the <a href={`${base}ctf/`}>Termux CTF page</a>. The installer prints everything it is about to write and waits for you to agree.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong><code>ctf</code> responds</strong>
          <span>Run <code>ctf list</code>. If the command is not found, open a new Termux session so the shell picks up the updated search path.</span>
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p class="ctf-empty">
        A fresh install has no challenge files on disk. Each one is written when you start
        it, and removed when you reset it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Command reference</h2>
      <p>These four are the whole game. Every challenge is played with them.</p>
      <table class="ctf-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Command</th>
            <th>What it does</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {coreCommands.map((c) => (
            <tr>
              <td><code>{c.cmd}</code></td>
              <td>{c.what}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <p>Three more exist for the moments the four above do not cover.</p>
      <table class="ctf-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Command</th>
            <th>What it does</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {extraCommands.map((c) => (
            <tr>
              <td><code>{c.cmd}</code></td>
              <td>{c.what}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h3>Arguments</h3>
      <p>
        Wherever a command takes an <code>&lt;id&gt;</code>, the number and the name are
        interchangeable. <code>ctf start 01</code> and <code>ctf start hidden-file</code>
        do the same thing, so you can use whichever is easier to type.
      </p>

      <h3>Exit codes</h3>
      <p>
        <code>ctf check</code> exits <code>0</code> when the flag is right and
        <code>1</code> when it is not, so a script can branch on the result.
        <code>ctf doctor</code> exits <code>1</code> if anything is wrong with the
        installation. Every other command exits <code>0</code>.
      </p>

      <h3>The menu</h3>
      <p>
        Running <code>ctf</code> with no arguments opens a numbered menu instead of
        printing a wall of text, because typing on a phone is the worst part of any
        terminal tool. It is the same seven commands, and it stays open until you quit.
        Piped into another command it prints the reference text instead, so a script never
        ends up waiting for input that will not arrive.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>A worked example</h2>
      <p>
        The first challenge, from beginning to end. Nothing here is specific to it: the
        other five follow exactly the same shape.
      </p>

      <h3>1. See what is on offer</h3>
      <Cmd cmd="ctf list" />
      <div class="ctf-banner">
        <pre>  ────────────────────────────────────────────────
    01   hidden-file          Easy     10 pts  open
    02   owner-only           Easy     15 pts  open
  ────────────────────────────────────────────────
    0 of 6 captured, 0 of 120 points</pre>
      </div>
      <p>
        The last column is the only one that changes as you play. It reads
        <code>open</code> until you capture a challenge and <code>captured</code>
        afterwards.
      </p>

      <h3>2. Read the briefing and set it up</h3>
      <Cmd cmd="ctf start 01" />
      <div class="ctf-banner">
        <pre>Two files were written into your home directory before you started.

Both have names that begin with a dot. A plain listing leaves those out,
which is why a directory can look emptier than it is.

One of the two holds your flag. The other is noise.

  Ready. 2 file(s) are in place in your home directory.</pre>
      </div>
      <p>
        The briefing teaches the concept the challenge is testing. It deliberately stops
        short of the answer, and it does not name the files it wrote.
      </p>

      <h3>3. Find the flag</h3>
      <p>
        This part is yours. The briefing points at the tool, and the hint narrows the
        search further if you want it. A plain <code>ls</code> shows nothing here, which is
        the first half of the lesson.
      </p>
      <Cmd cmd="ls -a" />
      <Cmd cmd="cat .backup-52b09ac4" note="The name is derived from your device, so yours will differ." />

      <h3>4. Hand it in</h3>
      <Cmd cmd="ctf check 01 LEARMUX&#123;...&#125;" note="The tool decides. It is the only thing holding your seed." />
      <div class="ctf-banner">
        <pre>  LEARMUX&#123;3f2a91c4b7e05d18&#125;
  Correct. 01 captured, 10 points.

  10 of 120 points.  ctf list to see what is left.</pre>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What a flag looks like</h2>
      <p>
        Every flag has the same shape: the word <code>LEARMUX</code>, an opening brace,
        sixteen hexadecimal characters, and a closing brace. Twenty-five characters in
        total. There is no whitespace inside one, and nothing about it is case-insensitive.
      </p>
      <div class="ctf-banner">
        <pre>LEARMUX&#123;3f2a91c4b7e05d18&#125;
        └──────────────┘
        16 hex characters</pre>
      </div>
      <p>
        Knowing the shape is useful when a flag is rejected. If what you copied does not
        match, the problem is almost always a partial copy rather than a wrong answer.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>When something goes wrong</h2>
      <table class="ctf-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Symptom</th>
            <th>What to do</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>A flag is rejected</td>
            <td>
              Check the shape first: twenty-five characters, no spaces, nothing missing
              from either end. If it looks right and is still refused, it is not the flag.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>The files are not there</td>
            <td>
              You may have reset or edited them past the point where they make sense.
              <code>ctf reset &lt;id&gt;</code> puts a challenge back to how it started, and
              your captures are untouched.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>The briefing went past too fast</td>
            <td>
              Run <code>ctf start &lt;id&gt;</code> again. It reprints everything and changes
              nothing, so it is always safe.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>You are stuck</td>
            <td>
              <code>ctf hint &lt;id&gt;</code> reveals one hint at a time and remembers how
              many you have seen, so asking again moves you forward rather than starting
              over.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Something behaves oddly</td>
            <td>
              <code>ctf doctor</code> checks the installation and names what is wrong. Run
              it before anything else.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>ctf</code> is not found</td>
            <td>
              Open a new Termux session so the shell reloads its search path, or run the
              installer again. It is safe to run more than once, and your progress stays.
            </td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Where everything is kept</h2>
      <p>
        The tool, your progress and your hint counts live in <code>~/.learmux</code>. The
        command itself is installed into <code>$PREFIX/bin</code>, which is already on your
        search path in Termux. Challenge files are written into your home directory, the
        way any other file would be.
      </p>
      <p>
        Every path the tool creates is recorded in a manifest as it goes. That is what
        lets the uninstaller remove exactly those paths and nothing else, so anything you
        put in your home directory yourself stays where it is.
      </p>
      <Cmd cmd="./uninstall.sh" note="Prints the full list of what it will remove, then asks." />
      <p>
        <a class="btn" href={`${base}ctf/challenges/`}>See the challenges</a>
        <a class="btn" href={`${base}ctf/progress/`}>Show your progress here</a>
        <a class="btn" href={ctfRepo} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the source</a>
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
